      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The stopping potential $V_s$ is directly related to the maximum kinetic energy by the work-energy theorem.",
      "evidence": "$K_{max} = eV_s$, where $e$ is the elementary charge.",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Combining the equations yields a linear relationship between stopping potential and frequency.",
      "evidence": "Substituting $K_{max}$ gives $eV_s = hf - \\phi$, or $V_s = (h/e)f - \\phi/e$. This is a linear function of $f$ with slope $h/e$ and y-intercept $-\\phi/e$.",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The work function is a constant property of the metal, not dependent on frequency or wavelength.",
      "evidence": "The work function $\\phi$ is the minimum energy required to remove an electron from the surface, determined by the material's atomic structure.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The photoelectric effect only occurs when the incident frequency exceeds the threshold frequency.",
      "evidence": "Electrons are emitted only if $hf > \\phi$, i.e., $f > f_0 = \\phi/h$. For $f < f_0$, no current flows regardless of intensity.",
